@module_doc("""
The JSON string codec.

Needs `import json`, which puts these in scope bare (`json_stringify(…)`),
qualified (`json.json_stringify(…)`) and as methods
(`x.json_stringify(…)`).
""")

# Package `json` (core/src/builtins/json.rs). A bundled builtin package: the
# petal runtime registers these natives in every Env, and the import is what
# puts them in scope.
#
# Native interface declarations: each `native fn` binds a name to the host
# native of the same name and gives the checker its parameter names, arity
# and types. Several declarations of one name are that native's call forms.
# See docs/usage/ffi.md (Native interfaces).

@doc("""
Encodes a value as JSON text: `nil` → `null`, bools, numbers, strings, lists
→ arrays, records → objects (field order kept). The one-argument form is
compact; `indent` pretty-prints with that many spaces per level. A class
instance encodes as its fields; a value with no JSON form (a function)
encodes as its display string. A value that is still loading — or holds one
anywhere inside it, like a record with a pending field — has no text yet, so
the result is that pending value rather than a string with a placeholder in
it. This is the codec for `panel_store_set` and any other string-only
channel.
""",
  params: {value: "The value to encode.", indent: "Spaces per level; leave off for compact output."},
  returns: "A string of JSON.",
  example: """
    import json
    json_stringify({a: 1, b: [true, nil]})   # {"a":1,"b":[true,null]}
    json_stringify("q\"uote")                # "q\"uote"
  """,
  see: ["json_parse"])
pub native fn json_stringify(value, indent?)

@doc("""
Decodes JSON text into the matching value — objects become plain records —
or returns `nil` when the text is not JSON, so a corrupt store entry is a
`?? default` rather than an aborted frame. (The text `null` also parses to
`nil`.)

```petal ignore
import json
import garden
state todos = json_parse(panel_store_get("todos") ?? "[]") ?? []
panel_store_set("todos", json_stringify(todos))
```
""",
  returns: "The decoded value, or `nil` when `text` is not JSON.",
  example: """
    import json
    json_parse("[1, 2.5, \"x\"]")        # [1, 2.5, "x"]
    json_parse("\{\"a\": \{\"b\": 2}}").a.b   # 2  (`\{` — a bare `{` interpolates)
    json_parse("not json")             # nil
  """,
  see: ["json_stringify"])
pub native fn json_parse(text)
